WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.454
How cool would it be

00:00:01.454 --> 00:00:05.521
if suddenly little lights&nbsp;would 
flow down from the ceiling,

00:00:05.521 --> 00:00:08.614
opening and closing, interacting with us

00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:09.900
like flowers?

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.957
Project&nbsp;"Shylight" became a process of years.

00:00:12.957 --> 00:00:17.071
We had to learn to build control boards, 
develop programming&nbsp;software,

00:00:17.071 --> 00:00:19.456
make mechanics move in an organic way 

00:00:19.456 --> 00:00:21.763
with gravity and complex silk patterns.

00:00:21.763 --> 00:00:23.625
We had to&nbsp;become engineers,

00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:24.719
programmers,

00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:25.983
seamstresses

00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:28.251
and choreographers at the same time.

00:00:28.251 --> 00:00:31.438
People responded&nbsp;
to them with wonder

00:00:31.438 --> 00:00:33.281
and a smile on their face,

00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:36.341
. It's not about that it moves.

00:00:37.049 --> 00:00:38.923
It's about how it&nbsp;moves.

00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:41.026
We are designed to be in a constant

00:00:41.026 --> 00:00:43.757
and dynamic interaction with our environment.

00:00:43.757 --> 00:00:45.896
Our&nbsp;body responds automatically

00:00:45.896 --> 00:00:47.939
to certain types of movement,

00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:50.284
movement we already know from nature.

00:00:50.284 --> 00:00:53.820
So why are human-built 
environments static?

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.813
Nothing moves.

00:00:55.813 --> 00:00:59.934
Do we realize that&nbsp;this also 
stops us from moving?

